\description{
Picks, one at a time, the explanatory variables that best explain a
community, by redundancy analysis of a count table (\code{\link[=rda]{rda()}}) or
distance-based redundancy analysis of distances (\code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}). At each step
the variable that raises the adjusted \eqn{R^2} most is added, if a
permutation test of it (given the variables already chosen) is
significant, and if the adjusted \eqn{R^2} stays within that of all the
candidates together: the double stopping rule of Blanchet et al. (2008),
as in \code{vegan::ordiR2step()}, with the same results. Usually run before
\code{\link[=varpart]{varpart()}}.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}

With the variables chosen so far, \eqn{S} (and any \code{Condition()} terms,
\eqn{Z}), each remaining candidate \eqn{t} gives a model \eqn{S + t},
whose adjusted \eqn{R^2} is Ezekiel's, \eqn{1 - (1 - R^2)(n - 1)/(n - m
- 1)} for \eqn{n} samples and \eqn{m} model columns (with conditions, the
adjusted \eqn{R^2} of \eqn{Z + S + t} less that of \eqn{Z}, as
\code{vegan::RsquareAdj()} gives it). The best candidate is added if:
\enumerate{
\item its adjusted \eqn{R^2} is higher than that of \eqn{S};
\item its adjusted \eqn{R^2} is no higher than that of the global model,
with every candidate (skipped if the global model has no residual
degrees of freedom);
\item its permutation test has a p-value of at most \code{alpha}; and
\item fewer than \code{max_vars} variables have been chosen.
}

Otherwise selection stops. The test of rule 3 is that of vegan's
\code{anova()} of the two nested models, \eqn{Z + S} and \eqn{Z + S + t}:
the pseudo-\eqn{F} of the sum of squares that \eqn{t} adds, over the
residual of the larger model, where for each permutation each model's
sum of squares is recomputed with the samples permuted (after removing
\eqn{Z}'s effect, as in \code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}}). Without \code{Condition()} terms this
is a plain permutation of the samples. (It differs slightly from a test
of \code{~ t + Condition(S + Z)}, which permutes the residuals of \eqn{S}
too.) The global model is tested as \code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}} tests it. Every test
uses the same permutations.

Rules 1 to 3 are \code{ordiR2step()}'s; rule 2
guards against the inflated type I error and overestimated explained
variation of forward selection (Blanchet et al. 2008).

\strong{Speed}

One pass over the table's non-zeros (or the distances) gives the cross
products of every candidate's columns, from which every candidate's
adjusted \eqn{R^2}, at every step, follows without another pass (as in
\code{\link[=varpart]{varpart()}}). Only the chosen candidate of each step is tested, with the
permutation kernel of \code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}}, in parallel, reading the distances
as a packed triangle; for RDA, the Euclidean distances among the
(normalized) samples, which give the same test. Results are identical
for any number of \code{cpus}.

\strong{Differences from vegan::ordiR2step()}
\itemize{
\item The result is a data frame of the steps, not the final model; the
steps' table is close to \code{ordiR2step()}'s \code{anova} component.
\item The global model is tested first, by default (\code{global_test}).
\item \code{max_vars} limits the number of variables.
\item There is no CCA (\code{ordiR2step()} on \code{cca()} models); use RDA of a
Hellinger-normalized table instead.
\item vegan's \code{ordiR2step()} chooses the scope from a formula with the
table on its left; here the community is \code{x}, and \code{formula} lists the
candidates.
}
}
\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation. If
\code{norm = 'clr'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}).

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\examples{
    # 40 samples: four environmental variables, two of which matter.
    set.seed(1)
    meta <- data.frame(
      ph    = runif(40, 5, 8),
      temp  = runif(40, 10, 20),
      depth = runif(40),
      site  = factor(rep(c('a', 'b', 'c', 'd'), 10)) )
    counts <- matrix(rpois(40 * 15, 4), 40, 15)
    counts[, 1:5] <- counts[, 1:5] + round(10 * (meta$ph - 5))
    counts[, 6:9] <- counts[, 6:9] + round(2 * (meta$temp - 10))

    # tb-RDA on the Hellinger-normalized table.
    fs <- forward_select(counts, ~ ph + temp + depth + site, meta, norm = 'hellinger')
    fs

    # db-RDA on Bray-Curtis distances, then partition the variation.
    fs <- forward_select(bray(counts), ~ ph + temp + depth + site, meta)
    attr(fs, 'selected')
    varpart(bray(counts), env = attr(fs, 'selected'), site = ~ site, data = meta)

    # Conditioned on site.
    forward_select(bray(counts), ~ ph + temp + depth + Condition(site), meta)

}
\references{
Blanchet, F. G., Legendre, P., & Borcard, D. (2008). Forward selection
of explanatory variables. \emph{Ecology}, 89, 2623-2632.
\doi{10.1890/07-0986.1}
}
